# secrets/signing.template — commit signing on a new machine.
#
# Signing is SSH-based on BOTH macOS and Linux, with the private key held in a Proton
# Pass vault and served to git by the Proton Pass SSH agent. Nothing key-specific is
# ever committed: the repo tracks only the always-sign policy
# (commit.gpgsign / tag.gpgsign in configs/common/git/common.gitconfig), and
# ~/.gitconfig auto-includes the per-machine file you create below.
#
# Missing that file => commits are BLOCKED rather than silently unsigned (fail-closed).
#
# ── Steps on a new machine ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. Install Proton Pass (a paid plan — SSH keys are not in Pass Essentials) and
#    enable its SSH agent in the app's settings.
#
# 2. Get a key into the vault. Either generate it there, or generate locally and
#    store it, keeping only the PUBLIC half on disk:
#        ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "git-signing <host>" -f ~/.ssh/git_signing_ed25519
#    git references the .pub; the agent provides the private half while the vault is
#    unlocked, so the private file can be removed once the vault holds it.
#
# 3. Point SSH_AUTH_SOCK at the agent. The socket is
#        ~/.ssh/proton-pass-ssh-agent.sock
#    Linux: already handled — configs/linux/environment.d/ssh-agent.conf covers shells
#    and systemd, and hyprland.lua sets the same for GUI apps it launches.
#    macOS: add to ~/.config/zsh/os.zsh (or the app's own agent setting, if it exposes
#    one), since there is no environment.d there.
#
# 4. Create ~/.config/git/signing.local (NOT tracked):
#
#        [gpg]
#        	format = ssh
#        [user]
#        	signingkey = /absolute/path/to/git_signing_ed25519.pub
#        [gpg "ssh"]
#        	allowedSignersFile = /absolute/path/to/.config/git/allowed_signers
#
#    Use ABSOLUTE paths — git does not reliably expand ~ in these values.
#
# 5. Create ~/.config/git/allowed_signers. Without it `git log --show-signature`
#    cannot verify anything locally; it is one line:
#
#        <your-git-email> <contents of the .pub>
#
#    e.g.  30913292+yurchenkoy@users.noreply.github.com ssh-ed25519 AAAA... comment
#
# 6. Add the PUBLIC key to GitHub as a **Signing key** (its own type — an
#    Authentication key does not make commits show as Verified).
#
# ── Checking it works ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#     ssh-add -l                       # the agent must list the key
#     git commit --allow-empty -m test && git log --show-signature -1
#
# "Good git signature" means the whole chain is wired.
#
# ── When it breaks ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#     error: No private key found for public key ".../git_signing_ed25519.pub"?
#
# means Proton Pass is not running or the vault is locked — the socket file can still
# exist while nothing listens on it (`ssh-add -l` then says "Connection refused").
# Unlock Proton Pass. This recurs after every reboot.
#
# Per-machine git config that is NOT signing (e.g. the gh credential helper) belongs in
# ~/.config/git/config, git's XDG global — ~/.gitconfig is tracked and gets overwritten.
